import "core"
import "reflect"


# # Memory
  The [Memory] is designed to work as part of [Context].
  The fundamental idea of [Memory] is change.
  It can best be pictured by a visualization where you see the program state with a slider on the bottom.
  Moving the slider forwards moves time forwards and shows later values of the memory.
  Moving it backwards will show earlier values of the memory.
  If this isn't the way you would picture and understand the application, it is probably not a good fit for the task.
  It also enables imperative code.
  Imperative code is defined by the usage of [Memory].
  However, [Memory] is not a fundamental aspect and shouldn't be built-in.
  Instead, using it as a library helps to bridge it with other concepts, but still enables the same programming strategies as imperative languages.
module(Data)
  class(Memory)
    # [Memory] is a state put in a [Context] to enable memory-based programming. A function using
      it names the state in its braces and answers with the state it leaves behind, so each
      change is a new value of the state, and the binds of a body thread it from one statement to
      the next.

# TODO A function should name the [Memory] class in its braces (@{m -> Memory}@) and be given
#   whichever memory the context holds. A state is found by the name of the type it is written
#   with, so @{Memory}@ never finds a [MemoryMap]. Until a state can be found by its class, the
#   functions below name the [MemoryMap] they work on.
# TODO [imperative] (@imperative{}(f{Memory} -> $R) -> $R@) adds a [MemoryMap] for the length of a
#   block when none is in the context and removes it afterwards. It needs a state added within a
#   scope and removed when it ends, and a function taking a context function as an argument, and
#   neither is supported yet.
module(Data/Memory)
  data(Var(id -> Integer, type -> /Catln/Type))
    # A [Var] is a variable within a [Memory]. If you think of the [Memory] as a map, then the
      [Var] is a key. It carries the [type] its values must be of, a [/Catln/Type] written as
      [/Catln/typeOf] of the type, which is checked whenever a value is put in it. The type is a
      value rather than a parameter of [Var], so what [read] gives back is of no one type the
      program can see, and it is read by arms for each shape it may be.

  class(MemoryErr, [MemoryErr/NotOf(target -> Var.., found)], isa= [/Data/Err])
    # [MemoryErr] is why a value was not put in a [Var]: it is not of the [Var]'s type ([NotOf]),
      and [found] is the value refused.

  # ## [MemoryMap]
  data(MemoryMap(map -> /Data/Map[$K -> Integer, $V -> /Data/Maybe], next -> Integer), isa= [/Data/Memory])
    # The [MemoryMap] is one of the simplest [Memory]s, one that keeps each [Var]'s value in a
      [Map] from its [id]: [Nothing] while the [Var] has no value, and [Some] of the value once
      it has one. [next] is the [id] the next [Var] is given. While most [Memory] are built using
      things like hardware or external services, this helps make sure that [Memory] can be used
      wherever. An empty one is [MemoryMap(map= /Data/Map/Leaf, next= 0)], the state a body adds to
      its context to start using memory.

  # [var] creates a new [Var] of a type, with no value yet. [read] is the value of a [Var]: [Some]
  #   of what it holds, or [Nothing] when it holds nothing or has been freed. [:=] puts a value in
  #   a [Var], replacing the one before, and answers [Ok], or fails with [NotOf] and leaves the
  #   [Var] as it was when the value is not of its type. [free] removes a [Var], reclaiming its
  #   place, and [varCount] is how many [Var]s the memory holds.
  # A body using memory binds each of these in turn (test/Integration/code/memory.ct). Binding a
  #   result under the name [v] is to be avoided for now: a bind's continuation is called through a
  #   lambda whose parameter is named [v], and the type checker reads that parameter as the [v] the
  #   body bound.
  var{MemoryMap(map, next)}(type -> /Catln/Type)= (Var(id= next, type= type)){MemoryMap(map= /Data/Map/insert(m= map, key= next, value= /Data/Maybe/Nothing), next= next + 1)}

  read{MemoryMap(map, next)}(target= Var(id, type))= (storedValue(found= /Data/Map/get(m= map, key= id))){MemoryMap(map= map, next= next)}

  storedValue(found= /Data/Maybe/Some(value@stored))= stored
  storedValue(found= /Data/Maybe/Nothing)= /Data/Maybe/Nothing

  (l :: Var.. := r :: $V){MemoryMap(map, next)}= (assigned(target= l, found= r, ok= /Catln/isOf(value= r, type= varType(target= l)))){MemoryMap(map= assignedMap(map= map, target= l, found= r, ok= /Catln/isOf(value= r, type= varType(target= l))), next= next)}

  varType(target= Var(id, type))= type

  varId(target= Var(id, type))= id

  assigned(target, found, ok= True)= /Data/Result/Ok(value= ())
  assigned(target, found, ok= False)= /Data/Result/Fail(err= MemoryErr/NotOf(target= target, found= found))

  assignedMap(map, target, found, ok= True)= /Data/Map/insert(m= map, key= varId(target= target), value= /Data/Maybe/Some(value= found))
  assignedMap(map, target, found, ok= False)= map

  free{MemoryMap(map, next)}(target -> Var..)= (){MemoryMap(map= /Data/Map/remove(m= map, key= varId(target= target)), next= next)}

  varCount{MemoryMap(map, next)}= (/Data/Map/size(m= map)){MemoryMap(map= map, next= next)}
module(Data)
  :/Data/Memory/MemoryErr/NotOf(target, found).msg= "the value is not of the type the variable holds"

  > /Data/Memory/MemoryErr/NotOf(target= /Data/Memory/Var(id= 0, type= /Catln/typeOf[$T -> Integer]), found= "a").msg
    >>> the value is not of the type the variable holds
